      "answer": "<strong>Deep Technical Breakdown & Implementation:</strong><br>\nA core dump is a memory image of a crashed process (e.g. on <code>SIGSEGV</code> Segmentation Fault or <code>SIGABRT</code>) written to disk for post-mortem debugging.<br><br>\n<strong>Enabling Core Dumps:</strong>\n<pre><code># Enable unlimited core dump file size in session\nulimit -c unlimited\n\n# Configure kernel core pattern (/etc/sysctl.d/core.conf)\nkernel.core_pattern = /var/crash/core-%e-%p-%t</code></pre>\n<strong>Debugging with GDB:</strong>\n<pre><code>gdb /usr/local/bin/myapp /var/crash/core-myapp-1234-1695123456\n(gdb) bt full       # Prints detailed stack backtrace with local variables\n(gdb) info threads  # Lists all active threads at crash moment\n(gdb) thread 2      # Inspects specific thread call stack</code></pre>",
      "level": "junior",
      "source": "DevOps/SRE Production Master Bank",
